counterfactuals.output:
A thermoelectric conversion module comprising:

a flexible substrate having a first principal surface and a second principal surface opposite to the first principal surface, the flexible substrate being bendable or conformable to a curved or uneven installation surface without impairing electrical continuity of conductors provided thereon;

a plurality of thermoelectric conversion elements disposed on the flexible substrate, each of the thermoelectric conversion elements including at least one thermoelectric material portion configured to generate an electromotive force in response to a temperature difference, or to generate heating and cooling in response to energization;

a plurality of electrodes electrically connected to the thermoelectric conversion elements, the electrodes being arranged in a predetermined electrode arrangement direction along the flexible substrate, and being configured to electrically connect the thermoelectric conversion elements in series, in parallel, or in a combination thereof, such that a heat-flow direction through the thermoelectric conversion elements intersects, is substantially perpendicular to, or is otherwise defined relative to the electrode arrangement direction;

a heat-transfer portion disposed at a heat-receiving side of the thermoelectric conversion elements and configured to transfer heat from a heat source to the thermoelectric conversion elements;

a heat-dissipation portion disposed at a heat-releasing side of the thermoelectric conversion elements and configured to dissipate heat from the thermoelectric conversion elements to an ambient environment or to a cooling member;

wherein the flexible substrate has a thermal conductivity selected to suppress excessive heat spreading in the electrode arrangement direction while permitting heat transfer in a thickness direction sufficient to maintain a temperature difference across the thermoelectric conversion elements;

wherein at least one of the heat-transfer portion and the heat-dissipation portion has a thermal conductivity higher than that of the flexible substrate, thereby promoting heat introduction to, or heat removal from, the thermoelectric conversion elements;

and wherein the thermoelectric conversion module is configured to be used under a pressing stress applied in a thickness direction of the module, the pressing stress being sufficient to maintain thermal contact between the thermoelectric conversion elements and the heat-transfer portion or heat-dissipation portion, while being low enough to prevent mechanical damage, cracking, delamination, or permanent deformation of the flexible substrate, the electrodes, and the thermoelectric conversion elements.

In one embodiment, the plurality of thermoelectric conversion elements include p-type thermoelectric elements and n-type thermoelectric elements alternately arranged along the electrode arrangement direction, adjacent p-type and n-type thermoelectric elements being electrically connected by the electrodes. The electrodes are provided so that electric current flows successively through the p-type and n-type thermoelectric elements, while heat is transferred between opposed heat-transfer and heat-dissipation sides of the module.

The flexible substrate may comprise a resin film, an elastomeric sheet, a flexible ceramic sheet, a metal foil provided with an insulating layer, or a composite thereof. The substrate preferably has sufficient flexibility to follow deformation of an object to which the module is attached, and sufficient heat resistance to withstand operating temperatures of the thermoelectric conversion elements.

The heat-transfer portion and the heat-dissipation portion may comprise thermally conductive sheets, metal plates, graphite sheets, ceramic plates, thermal interface materials, fins, heat spreaders, or combinations thereof. At least one of said portions is preferably deformable or compliant so as to reduce thermal contact resistance under the applied pressing stress.

The pressing stress is preferably applied uniformly over an active thermoelectric region of the module, thereby reducing variation in thermal contact resistance among the plurality of thermoelectric conversion elements and improving conversion efficiency, output stability, and mechanical reliability.
